<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rakvere</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wesenberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rakvere, German Wesenberg, is a town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>. Near it on 18 February 1268 the armies of Novgorod and other Russian principalities fought the Livonian Order and the Danish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of northern Estonia in the bloody Battle of Wesenberg, which neither side won decisively. The town grew under Danish and Livonian rule around a castle whose ruins still stand.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rakvere.jpg" alt="Rakvere" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daviidos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rakvere_Estonia_View_over_the_city.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1268 Novgorod and its allies fought Danish and Livonian forces at <b>Rakvere</b> (Wesenberg).</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Near <b>Rakvere</b> in northern Estonia, an army of Novgorod and Pskov under Prince Dmitry of Pereslavl fought the forces of Danish Estonia and the Livonian Order.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-18">18 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It was one of the largest ancient Estonian lands, with several parishes and hillforts; its main stronghold, <b>Agelinde</b>, is usually identified with Rakvere.</blockquote><a href="/state/virumaa">Virumaa</a></li></ul></section>
